16. Sequence Control
Sequence control means statements execute in their normal order.
int a = 10;
int b = 20;
int c = a + b;
printf("%d", c);
Execution occurs from top to bottom.

17. Selection Statements

if
if(age &gt;= 18)
    printf("Adult");


if-else
if(mark &gt;= 50)
    printf("Pass");
else
    printf("Fail");


switch
switch(choice)
{
    case 1:
        printf("One");
        break;

    case 2:
        printf("Two");
        break;
}

18. Loop Statements

for
for(i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++)
{
    printf("%d", i);
}


while
while(i &lt; 10)
{
    i++;
}


do-while
do
{
    i++;
} while(i &lt; 10);

20. Structures
A structure groups variables of different types.
struct Student
{
    int roll;
    char name[50];
    float mark;
};
Create an object:
struct Student s1;

21. Union
A union allows its members to share the same memory area.
union Data
{
    int i;
    float f;
    char c;
};

Structure vs Union







Structure
Union




Members have separate storage
Members share storage


Multiple member values can coexist
Normally one member value is meaningful at a time


Size relates to combined member storage
Size is based mainly on the largest member

25. Parameter Passing

Call by Value
A copy of the value is passed.
Changes to the parameter do not directly change the original variable.


Address-based Modification
C can pass an address using pointers.
void change(int *x)
{
    *x = 100;
}
Call:
int a = 10;
change(&a);
Now a becomes 100.

30. Class
A class is a blueprint for creating objects.
class Student
{
public:
    int roll;

    void display()
    {
        cout &lt;&lt; roll;
    }
};

32. Instantiation
Instantiation means creating an object from a class.
Student s1;

33. Encapsulation
Encapsulation means combining data and functions into a single unit and controlling access to the data.
class Account
{
private:
    double balance;

public:
    void deposit(double amount)
    {
        balance += amount;
    }
};

35. Inheritance
Inheritance allows a new class to acquire properties and behavior from an existing class.
class Animal
{
};

class Dog : public Animal
{
};
Types:

Single
Multiple
Multilevel
Hierarchical
Hybrid

37. Abstract Class
An abstract class is intended to be used as a base class and may contain pure virtual functions.
class Shape
{
public:
    virtual void draw() = 0;
};
An abstract class cannot normally be instantiated directly.

40. Variables and Constants
Variable:
int age = 20;
Constant:
const int MAX = 100;

44. Virtual Functions
A virtual function supports run-time polymorphism.
class Animal
{
public:
    virtual void sound()
    {
        cout &lt;&lt; "Animal";
    }
};
A derived class can override the function.

45. Constructors
A constructor initializes an object.
It has the same name as the class and has no return type.
class Student
{
public:
    Student()
    {
        cout &lt;&lt; "Constructor";
    }
};

47. Function Overloading
Same function name with different parameter lists.
int add(int a, int b)
{
    return a + b;
}

float add(float a, float b)
{
    return a + b;
}
This is compile-time polymorphism.

48. Operator Overloading
Operators can be given special behavior for user-defined types.
Complex operator+(const Complex& c)
{
    ...
}

49. Templates
Templates support generic programming.
template &lt;class T&gt;
T maximum(T a, T b)
{
    return (a &gt; b) ? a : b;
}

50. Exception Handling
Main keywords:
try
catch
throw
Example:
try
{
    throw 10;
}
catch(int x)
{
    cout &lt;&lt; "Exception";
}

53. HTML
HTML means HyperText Markup Language.
It structures web pages.
&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
&lt;body&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;Hello&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Welcome.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
Common tags:
&lt;html&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
&lt;title&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
&lt;a&gt;
&lt;img&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;form&gt;

Roster Form
Elements are explicitly listed.
A = {2, 4, 6, 8}

Set-builder Form
A property is specified.
A = {x | x is an even positive integer less than 10}
